<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    plans/plan_xnapipeline_parity.md XNAPP-267: a description asking for glyphs the font has not
    got.

    Liberation Mono covers Latin; this asks for U+4E00..U+4E0F, which is CJK. Whether that is an
    error or a substitution is exactly what the case measures: a processor may refuse the region or
    fall back to <DefaultCharacter>, and the two builds have to make the same choice.

    The font name is the family, spelled as the face declares it, because the two sides resolve a
    font differently on purpose: XNA asks Windows for an installed family, CNA looks for a file
    beside the description so that a build produces the same bytes on every machine. The harness
    presents the same face to each in the form that side resolves; see control_font.spritefont.
-->
<XnaContent xmlns:Graphics="Microsoft.Xna.Framework.Content.Pipeline.Graphics">
  <Asset Type="Graphics:FontDescription">
    <FontName>Liberation Mono</FontName>
    <Size>14</Size>
    <Spacing>0</Spacing>
    <UseKerning>true</UseKerning>
    <Style>Regular</Style>
    <CharacterRegions>
      <CharacterRegion>
        <Start>&#x4E00;</Start>
        <End>&#x4E0F;</End>
      </CharacterRegion>
    </CharacterRegions>
  </Asset>
</XnaContent>
